\details{
Span processors are part of the OpenTelemetry SDK, not the API, so
they are meant to be set up by whoever configures the telemetry output
of an app, not by the authors of instrumented packages.

Span processors are global: they apply to the spans of all tracer
providers.

Currently span processors only support an \code{on_start} callback.
\subsection{\code{on_start}}{

The \code{on_start} callback is called right after a span has started, for
every span that is recording. It is called with two arguments:
\itemize{
\item \code{span}: the new span, an \code{otel_span} object. You can call its
methods, e.g. \code{span$set_attribute()} or \code{span$set_attributes()}.
\item \code{info}: a named list with more information about the span:
\itemize{
\item \code{name}: span name.
\item \code{tracer_name}: name of the tracer that created the span. This is
usually the name of the instrumented package.
\item \code{kind}: span kind, a string.
\item \code{parent}: the \code{parent} span option, if it was set explicitly when
starting the span, or \code{NULL}.
}
}

The return value of \code{on_start} is ignored.

If a callback fails, otelsdk emits an \code{otel_error_message} message,
and continues with the next span processor. The span is still created.
Set the \code{OTEL_ENV=dev} environment variable to get errors instead.

If a callback starts a new span, span processors are not called for
that span.
}

\subsection{Setting up span processors from an environment variable}{

Use the \code{OTEL_R_SPAN_PROCESSORS} environment variable to set up span
processors that are defined in packages, without changing the code of
the app. It is a comma separated list of \code{pkg::name} entries. Each
entry must refer to a named list of callbacks, e.g.:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode r">}}\preformatted{span_processor <- list(
  on_start = function(span, info) \{
    span$set_attribute("deployment.region", "eu-west-1")
  \}
)
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

and then

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode">}}\preformatted{OTEL_R_SPAN_PROCESSORS=mypkg::span_processor
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

otelsdk reads \code{OTEL_R_SPAN_PROCESSORS} when the first span is
started. The id of these span processors is \verb{env:} followed by the
entry, e.g. \code{env:mypkg::span_processor}.

If the list has callbacks that this version of otelsdk does not
support, otelsdk ignores them with a message, and uses the rest of the
span processor. This way a package can define callbacks for newer
otelsdk versions and still work with older ones.
}

\subsection{Example Shiny app}{

This span processor adds the user and the session token to every span
that starts inside a Shiny session:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode r">}}\preformatted{# Add the Shiny user and session token to every span that Shiny creates
# for this app. See README.md in this directory for how to run it.

library(shiny)

otelsdk::add_span_processor(
  id = "shiny-session",
  on_start = function(span, info) \{
    session <- shiny::getDefaultReactiveDomain()
    if (is.null(session)) \{
      return()
    \}
    user <- if (is.null(session$user)) "anonymous" else session$user
    span$set_attribute("app.user", user)
    span$set_attribute("app.session.token", session$token)
  \}
)

ui <- fluidPage(
  titlePanel("Span processor example"),
...
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

